-- Adversarial soundness probes.
-- Each probe is classified by running it twice: once with --check, once for real.
--
--   TYPECHECK_REJECT  --check exits nonzero (the type system caught it)
--   PANIC             --check passes, but running exits nonzero
--   CLEAN_RUN         --check passes and running exits zero
--
-- The point is not that a probe "passes" — it is that its classification never
-- silently downgrades. A TYPECHECK_REJECT that becomes a PANIC means the type
-- system stopped catching something. A PANIC that becomes a CLEAN_RUN is worse:
-- it means a program that should have crashed now returns a wrong answer.
--
-- FORMAT: -- EXPECT: <classification>
-- <code, one or more lines, until the next EXPECT>

-- ===== linear discipline: boxes must be consumed exactly once =====

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- box is linear: not copyable
42 box dup

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- box is linear: drop is for stackables
42 box drop

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- box is linear: must be consumed, not abandoned
42 box

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- consumed twice
42 box dup free free

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- free rejects dicts at type time
dict free

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- the sanctioned lifecycle
42 box free

-- ===== protocol constraints =====

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- symbols are Eq-only, never Ord
'a 'b lt

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- sort needs Ord; symbols are not
['a 'b] sort

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- mod is Integral: floats are not
1.0 2.0 mod

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- no implicit int/float coercion
1 2.0 plus

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- symbols do support Eq
'a 'a eq drop

-- ===== either / tagged discipline =====

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- default's fallback must match the 'ok payload type: () is a tuple, the
-- list's elements are ints
[1 2 3] pop () default

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- ...and a string fallback cannot stand in for an int payload either
[1 2 3] pop "str" default len drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- the (()) idiom is fine when the payload really is a tuple
[(1 2) (3 4)] pop (()) default drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- a fallback of the right type is fine
[1 2 3] pop -1 default drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- the empty-list path takes the fallback
[] pop -1 default drop

-- ===== runtime panics: programmer errors that types cannot see =====

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- division by zero
1 0 div drop

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- modulo by zero
1 0 mod drop

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- must on a 'no value
none must drop

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- index out of bounds, unwrapped
[1 2 3] 99 get must drop

-- ===== fallible ops must not panic; they return tagged =====

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- out-of-bounds get is 'no, not a crash
[1 2 3] 99 get 0 default drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- take-n clamps rather than panicking
[1 2 3] 99 take-n drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- drop-n clamps rather than panicking
[1 2 3] 99 drop-n drop

-- ===== peek: the non-consuming indexed read =====

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- out-of-bounds peek is 'no and the list stays on the stack
[1 2 3] 99 peek 0 default drop drop

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- ...but peek must still panics
[1 2 3] 99 peek must drop drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- peek deep-copies the element, so dropping the extracted dict must not free the
-- one the list still holds. A shallow VCPY here is CRASH(sig 5).
dict "k" 1 insert list swap push 0 peek must drop drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- peek inside a lend body reads the snapshot without let-binding it (which the
-- lend guard would reject). lend leaves the box under both of peek's results.
[1 2 3] box (1 peek must) lend drop drop free

-- ===== heap identity: box and dict are pointers, everything else is copied =====

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- `nth` used to SPUSH the binding's storage verbatim, so the pushed dict and the
-- one still in the list shared a DictData. Dropping one freed the other's
-- entries and the next read aborted in malloc. Now it deep-copies.
dict "k" 1 insert list swap push 'l let
'l 0 nth must drop  'l 0 nth must dict-keys len 1 eq assert drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- same bug in `of`, which unlike dict-values and dict_push_kv_tuple pushed the
-- entry's storage without copying while the dict lived on
dict "k" 42 box 'x tag insert "k" of must swap drop 0 {'x (free)} case

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- a box whose contents point at itself is constructible through mutate. Both
-- deep_copy_values and deep_free_values used to recurse on it until the C stack
-- died (CRASH sig 11); now the walk is depth-bounded and says so.
42 box box 'b let b (drop b) mutate clone free free

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- read-only traversal of a boxed list. The lend guard used to reject this even
-- though BOX_UNPACK copies list content bitwise (expect.slap pins that), which
-- forced euler/23.slap to use `mutate` for a scan that changes nothing.
[1 2 3] box ('l let l 0 get must) lend swap free drop

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- ...while box-in-box, the case that really is aliased by pointer, used to slip
-- through and use-after-free with a misleading "double-free" message
42 box box 'ob let
ob ('inner let ob (99 box) mutate free inner free 1) lend swap free drop

-- ===== frame value arena =====

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- rebinding a name to a larger value abandoned its arena region every time, so
-- an accumulator loop leaked its whole history and died with "frame value
-- storage full" after ~500 turns. Now a binding that owns the top of the arena
-- grows in place.
"" 0 (dup 4000 lt) ('gi let 'gacc let gacc "y" cat gi 1 plus) while drop len 4000 eq assert

-- ===== still open =====

-- EXPECT: TYPECHECK_REJECT
-- a let-bound dict aliases the heap object, so `d drop` frees it and the binding
-- dangles -- and `of` then returned a wrong answer read out of freed memory
-- rather than failing. kv-server.slap:14 documented the stack-threading
-- discipline in a comment; now the checker enforces it.
dict "a" "b" insert 'd let d drop d "a" of must drop

-- EXPECT: CLEAN_RUN
-- KNOWN-GAP: insert's linear guard tests the top-level AT_LINEAR flag only, so
--   wrapping a box in a tag launders it into a stackable dict.
-- GAP-TARGET: TYPECHECK_REJECT
dict "k" 42 box 'x tag insert drop

-- EXPECT: PANIC
-- KNOWN-GAP: bare Box bindings are treated as mutable references and are not
--   single-use checked; the double-consume is left to the runtime poison.
-- GAP-TARGET: TYPECHECK_REJECT
42 box 'b let b free b free
